  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 404 AM MST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nMidlevel flow becomes more southwesterly today ahead of an \nadvancing trough along the West Coast. This will lead to another\nwarm and dry day, even warmer than yesterday for most with \nhighs peaking near 10 degrees above normal. The Wind Corridor \nfrom eastern Sweetwater County through Natrona County will see a\nfamiliar southwesterly breeze with gusts to 35 mph.\n\nThe trough will approach Tuesday as an open wave. Most guidance\nstill has this digging south into the Great Basin and splitting\nsomewhat as it pulls northward into the Rockies. While this \ndoes not favor a particularly noteworthy precipitation event, \nforecast totals have increased as of late for western Wyoming. \nRain and mountain snow will begin late Tuesday morning, \nspreading eastward through Tuesday night. Locations east of the \nDivide can expect another warm and windy day ahead of an \nassociated cold front. This front will bring snow levels down to\nthe western valleys Tuesday night. Currently total liquid \namounts are in the 0.50-0.70\" range across the western mountains\nand about half as much in the valleys. This should be good \nenough for 4 to 8 inches in the Tetons, Gros Ventres, and \nwestern Wind River Mountains, with most of this falling before \nsunrise Wednesday. Western valleys can expect an inch or two of \nsnow accumulation during this period. \n\nMeanwhile, wind will ramp up further Tuesday night east of the \nDivide as a surface low develops across northern Wyoming ahead \nof the advancing cold front. This will lead to gusts of 50 to 60\nmph across the more susceptible areas of the Wind Corridor, \nthough gusts of 35 to 45 will be more widespread elsewhere. The \nbest chance for precipitation east of the Divide will be Tuesday\nnight through Wednesday morning. The Bighorns will be most \nfavored here with 0.25- 0.50\" of liquid expected; lower \nelevations will generally see under a tenth of an inch. Snow \nlevels may drop just low enough for light snow across the basins\nWednesday morning, though any accumulation is expected to be \nminimal.\n\nPrecipitation is largely expected to end by Wednesday afternoon. A \ncooler airmass will be in place, with temperatures peaking around \nmore seasonable values. Most models have the next system following \nquickly, with precipitation returning to western Wyoming beginning \nThursday morning. This next system appears to progress similarly to \nthe former, though perhaps a little stronger in magnitude. The \ncurrent forecast reflects this, with more widespread precipitation \nchances for the area from Thursday evening through Saturday morning. \nWith the colder air in place, this could bring light snow to the \nbasins on both sides of the Divide. While uncertainty in the \nprogression and strength of this system remains high, continued \ncooler and unsettled weather is likely through the end of the\nweek.",
